      "q": "17. Titanium shows a magnetic moment of $1.73{\\text{ BM}}$ in its compound. What is the oxidation number of ${\\text{Ti}}$ in the compound?\nAns.\nThe formula for finding the magnetic moment is: $\\mu\u00a0 = \\sqrt {n(n + 2)} {\\text{ BM}}$. The value of magnetic moment $(\\mu )$ is $1.73{\\text{ BM}}$. So, it will have one unpaired electron.\nThe electronic configuration of titanium is ${{\\text{[Ar]}}^{{\\text{18}}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{, 4}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}$. So, titanium will lose three electrons, so that it will have one unpaired electron left. Thus, the oxidation number will be $ + 3$.",
